1 REFN georges2_t
2 TYPE project-id
1 NAME Georges /Thumas/
2 GIVN Georges
2 SURN Thumas
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 24 JAN 1804
2 PLAC Grez-Doiceau
1 DEAT
2 DATE 12 JAN 1864
2 PLAC Grez-Doiceau
1 OCCU menuisier (carpenter)
1 FAMS @F126@
1 FAMC @F115@
1 NOTE Third of the four carpenter generations. Civil records give his birth as 24 Jan 1804 (earlier trees said 1805).
2 CONT
2 CONT CORROBORATED (July 2026) as a father, by his son's Kraainem marriage act of 20 June 1872,
2 CONT which names the bridegroom's parents as Georges Thumas and Maria Catharina Joostens — this
2 CONT couple. He appears again in his wife's 1857 death act ([[S9]]) as her surviving husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT ACT READ (July 2026): named as the bridegroom's father in the Kraainem act of 20 June 1872,
2 CONT with [[joostens]] as the mother — the same couple his wife's 1857 death act shows.
2 CONT
2 CONT AGE CONFLICT (July 2026), recorded and left open. His wife's death act of 10 June 1857
2 CONT names him as her surviving husband — *Georges Thumas, menuisier of Grez-Doiceau*, the trade
2 CONT this record already carries, so the identification is not in doubt. But it gives his age as
2 CONT **63**, which puts his birth around 1793–94. This record says **24 January 1804**, which
2 CONT would have made him 53.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ten years is too wide to be the usual slippage, and the same act gets his wife's age right —
2 CONT 47, agreeing with her recorded 1810. So one of the two is wrong and it is not obvious which:
2 CONT the 1804 date has never been documented, and the 63 was spoken by whoever reported the death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nothing changed. FRONTIER: the Grez-Doiceau baptisms for 1793–94 and for 1804, and his own
2 CONT 1864 death act, which would state an age to compare.
2 CONT
2 CONT REFINED (2026-07-26) — **the parent link does decide something after all, and the earlier
2 CONT note here missed it.** It compared only his father's *birth* year, 1772, and concluded that
2 CONT fatherhood at 22 or at 32 is equally ordinary. But [[georgeslambert_t]] married
2 CONT [[quinart]] on **9 February 1801**. A son born in 1793–94 would arrive *seven years before
2 CONT his parents' marriage*; a son born in January 1804 arrives three years after it, which is
2 CONT unremarkable. That does not prove 1804 — births before a marriage happen, and this couple's
2 CONT marriage date itself rests on Geneanet and is only `sup` — but it moves the weight decidedly
2 CONT onto 1804 and away from the 63.
2 CONT
2 CONT It also matters *whose* age the 1857 act was reporting. The figure it got right, 47, was the
2 CONT **deceased's**: the person the act exists to record, checked against her own papers. The 63
2 CONT was the age of a **surviving spouse**, stated in passing by whoever came to report the death.
2 CONT Those are not equally reliable numbers, and the act getting one right says little about the
2 CONT other.
2 CONT
2 CONT VENUES CLOSED (2026-07-26), so this is not a frontier to re-walk at AGATHA:
2 CONT - **his 1864 death act** — Grez-Doiceau deaths are indexed nearly completely for 1860 (49
2 CONT   acts) and then only in a trickle: about two to four a year for 1861–1874. No Georges Thumas
2 CONT   is among them. All eighteen Thumas appearances in Grez-Doiceau death acts of 1860–1880 are
2 CONT   *Charles Julien Thumas as* **Belanghebbende** — a commune official who turns up in dozens of
2 CONT   acts, and a standing trap for any match made on surname and place alone.
2 CONT - **his 1804 birth act** — AGATHA's Grez-Doiceau birth acts cover **1813 and 1814 only** (57
2 CONT   acts, all of them in those two years). 1804 is not in the index at all.
2 CONT
2 CONT What is left is the register images themselves — FamilySearch's Brabant-wallon civil
2 CONT registration for 1804, and the parish registers for 1793–94.
